<section id="timeline" class="card" aria-labelledby="timeline-heading">
  <div class="card-header">
    <h2 id="timeline-heading">Timeline</h2>
  </div>
  <div class="card-body">
    <ol class="list-unstyled position-relative ps-4 mb-0" style="border-left: 3px solid #a59e86;">
      <li class="mb-4 ps-3">
        <h3 class="h5 mb-1"><time datetime="2014">2014</time> — The problem</h3>
        <p class="mb-0">A <a href="https://boilerinvasion.org" target="_blank" rel="noopener">FIRST Robotics team</a> couldn’t keep its information straight. Attendance, fundraising progress, and competition logistics were scattered across spreadsheets, sticky notes, and the heads of whoever happened to be available to ask. Leadership needed one place everyone could find.</p>
      </li>
      <li class="mb-4 ps-3">
        <h3 class="h5 mb-1"><time datetime="2014-09">Fall 2014 – 2015</time> — The first fix</h3>
        <p class="mb-0"><a href="https://brad-thompson.com" target="_blank" rel="noopener">One volunteer web developer</a> built a small internal tool to pull it together — just enough to get through the season. It handled roster, calendar, and basic bookkeeping for a single organization.</p>
      </li>
      <li class="mb-4 ps-3">
        <h3 class="h5 mb-1"><time datetime="2015">2015 – 2024</time> — Ten years in production</h3>
        <p class="mb-0">That tool became the operational backbone of <a href="https://westside-robotics.info" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Westside Robotics</a>, an organization that quadrupled in size over the decade. What started as a quick fix grew into daily infrastructure: time-clock kiosk with PIN/barcode/RFID, tagged ledger with Stripe, team-scoped announcements, and custom reports — all shaped by real volunteer feedback.</p>
      </li>
      <li class="mb-4 ps-3">
        <h3 class="h5 mb-1"><time datetime="2024">2024</time> — The rebuild as GatherPack</h3>
        <p class="mb-0">When the original codebase showed its age, the same developer and a few students rebuilt it from scratch as GatherPack — this time as general group management software flexible enough for a 10-person club or a 500-person nonprofit, and open source under the <a href="https://github.com/GatherPack/gatherpack/blob/main/LICENSE" target="_blank" rel="noopener">MIT license</a> (SPDX: <code>MIT</code>). Same lessons, broader audience.</p>
      </li>
      <li class="mb-0 ps-3">
        <h3 class="h5 mb-1"><time datetime="2025">2025 – 2026</time> — In production, in the open</h3>
        <p class="mb-0">GatherPack runs <time datetime="2024">since 2024</time> as GatherPack (predecessor <time datetime="2014">since 2014</time>). Two organizations are live, six more are evaluating. The source lives at <a href="https://github.com/GatherPack/gatherpack" target="_blank" rel="noopener">github.com/GatherPack/gatherpack</a>, issues and discussions on <a href="https://discord.gg/r3fmNDVn3N" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Discord</a>. We keep counts conservative until a planned opt-in “register your server” phone-home can share totals honestly. <a href="/updates/gatherpack-story-extended/">Read the extended story →</a></p>
      </li>
    </ol>
    <p class="small text-muted mt-4 mb-0">All milestones use <code>&lt;time datetime=""&gt;</code> for machine extraction. Founding: predecessor 2014, GatherPack 2024. Publisher: Sledgehammer Infosystems.</p>
  </div>
</section>

